<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Bevan, E. (1927) The House of Ptolemy: A History of Hellenistic Egypt under the Ptolemaic Dynasty.</li><li>Fischer-Bovet, C. (2015) &quot;The Ptolemaic Army&quot;, in Oxford Handbook Topics in Classical Studies. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</li><li>Hölbl, G. (2000) A History of the Ptolemaic Empire. Trans. T. Saavedra. Routledge.</li><li>Manning, J. G. (2003) Land and Power in Ptolemaic Egypt: The Structure of Land Tenure. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Peters, F. E. (1970) The Harvest of Hellenism. New York: Simon &amp; Schuster.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: a modern estimate for the kingdom at its height, by the late Ptolemaic period; the 3rd century BCE may have had somewhat fewer.</p></section>
